 <ul>
 <li><strong>Every well stands among the houses it serves, never out in the fields.</strong><br>On hamlet and town maps a well must have a dwelling within about 95 ft, and the map places its wells a little closer than that.<!-- wells_among_dwellings ~95 px; place_wells(near=85) --></li>
 <li><strong>Whether a village's wells stand by the lane or among the houses is a knob.</strong><br>Each settlement takes one of two forms, chosen from its seed. In the lane-side form the wellhead stands on a lane verge, clear of the tread, within reach of the houses; in the cluster form it stands in the open ground among five to ten houses, off every lane - the five to ten houses that each dug well served at Karihama, a coastal village in Ehime on Shikoku.<sup class="fn" data-note="seiyo-karihama-ido"></sup> The cluster form is this project's reading of that village; the lane-side form is attested for wayside wells in general,<sup class="fn" data-note="kotobank-tsujiido"></sup> and its use in a village is a GUESS.</li>
-<li><strong>How a clustered hamlet seats each form.</strong><br>Its lanes are drawn after its farmsteads, through the ground between them, so a lane-side well is seated once the lanes stand: on a lane's verge, its curb within about 8 ft of the tread. Where every verge is a yard, a bed or another lane, as it often is between a hamlet's farmsteads, the well takes the other form and stands in the open ground among the houses instead. A cluster well is seated among five houses, off every lane; how far round a seat the five may stand is this project's choice, widened before the count of five gives way, and only a hamlet with fewer than five households to share a well takes three, or two. A well is seated with a dwelling's wall within 80 ft, a little closer than the 95 ft every well must keep. The verge's width and the 80 ft are each a GUESS: no page gives either. Where neither form finds a seat, the well is seated among three houses, then two, at the 95 ft, a deviation from both forms.<!-- marked on the map's record --> Where no seat keeps every well to ten households, as when one well stands inside a farmstead, the well serving more stands as a deviation from the five to ten houses a Karihama well served.<!-- marked on the map's record --><!-- feature 374 wave 154 (ask:w154-lane-wells, hour:374-match-the-research-continued@3): m:w154-wells, m:w154-lane-wells; the lane form drawn on 1 of 5 lane rolls of the 44 --></li>
+<li><strong>How a clustered hamlet seats each form.</strong><br>Its lanes are drawn after its farmsteads, through the ground between them, so a lane-side well is seated once the lanes stand: on a lane's verge, its curb within about 8 ft of the tread. Where every verge is a yard, a bed or another lane, as it often is between a hamlet's farmsteads, the well takes the other form and stands in the open ground among the houses instead. A cluster well is seated among five houses, off every lane; how far round a seat the five may stand is this project's choice, widened before the count of five gives way, and only a hamlet with fewer than five households to share a well takes three, or two. A well is seated with a dwelling's wall within 80 ft, a little closer than the 95 ft every well must keep. The verge's width and the 80 ft are each a GUESS: no page gives either. Where neither form finds a seat, the well is seated among three houses, then two, at the 95 ft, a deviation from both forms.<!-- marked on the map's record --> No well stands inside a household's farmstead: every well a clustered hamlet owes is a shared well of its form. Where a household stands beyond the 760 ft reach of every well and of open water, or one well would be the nearest for more than ten households, one more well is seated by the same form, so a hamlet whose houses spread past two wells' reach, or crowd round one, draws more than the one or two above: still within the one well to 2-20 households the record counts for a hamlet.<!-- marked on the map's record --> Where even that leaves a well serving more than ten, it stands as a deviation from the five to ten houses a Karihama well served.<!-- marked on the map's record --><!-- feature 374 wave 154 (ask:w154-lane-wells, hour:374-match-the-research-continued@3): m:w154-wells, m:w154-lane-wells; the lane form drawn on 1 of 5 lane rolls of the 44 --></li>
 <li><strong>A communal well sits on a paved apron about 4 ft wider than its curb all round.</strong><br>The village of Karihama, in Ehime, paved the ground round many of its common wells;<sup class="fn" data-note="seiyo-karihama-ido"></sup> the apron's width is a GUESS.</li>
 <li><strong>No well is drawn out on open common ground.</strong><br>No page we read sets a village's well there.</li>
 <li><strong>A shrine or temple set apart from the houses keeps its own ablution well.</strong><br>Where its nearest dwelling is more than about 150 ft off, a well stands beside the hall, measured from the hall's edge, so a large hall pushes its well outward. It is a well rather than a ditch or a pond by the map's convention, not a finding: no page we read says which waters a shrine used.</li>
